{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-64593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=10\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-10{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98cf42d54a60f7a723f911cb6559c45a5b682c8792f174347b1c07fa504a0e41"} {"question" "q-64593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+3y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+9y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+18y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98cf42d54a60f7a723f911cb6559c45a5b682c8792f174347b1c07fa504a0e41"} {"question" "q-64617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-64617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98cf42d54a60f7a723f911cb6559c45a5b682c8792f174347b1c07fa504a0e41"} {"question" "q-94311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $14\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=28\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=14\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n14{y}^{″}=-14{y}^{′}-28y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98cf42d54a60f7a723f911cb6559c45a5b682c8792f174347b1c07fa504a0e41"} {"question" "q-94324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+7y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+2y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=1\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=2\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98cf42d54a60f7a723f911cb6559c45a5b682c8792f174347b1c07fa504a0e41"} {"question" "q-94359" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94359" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "\\pi \\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"} {"question" "q-94359" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071865/state.json" "file_sha256" "68c3bf8b584094befc95bb07724f635b8e1175fcf3abe9e270f600a3803a31e3"}] "usage" {"q-64617" [] "q-64593" []} "basis" 2588 "retractions" [[17592186148128 :question/problem "A particle of mass $2kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 18kg/s^{2}$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10kg/s$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?"] [17592186148130 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{″} + 3y^{′} + 7y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n2. $y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 8y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n3. $y^{″} + 8y^{′} + 16y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146780 17592186146781 17592186146782 17592186146783 17592186146784 17592186146792 17592186146793 17592186146794 17592186146795 17592186146796] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146779 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146779 :answer-field/id] [17592186146779 :answer-field/key] [17592186146779 :answer-field/type] [17592186146791 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146791 :answer-field/id] [17592186146791 :answer-field/key] [17592186146791 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
